{#
   A page's header, in two strips.

   Top strip: the controls, and the name of the current page. Bottom strip:
   the path that leads there. A single strip carried both for a while, the
   last level of the breadcrumb serving as the title; it was forty-eight
   pixels shorter, but the page's name read in the middle of a path rather
   than as a title.

   The two strips are therefore back, but tighter: forty-eight pixels for the
   title instead of sixty-four, thirty-two for the breadcrumb instead of
   forty, and the breadcrumb goes small since it is secondary navigation.
   Eighty pixels in total against a hundred and four.

   **The name is taken from the breadcrumb's last level, not passed
   separately.** Each page's name is already the last item of its path; asking
   the nineteen templates to repeat it in a `title` would be the same string
   written twice, which drifts at the first rewording. A page that wants a
   fuller name than its level allows still passes `title`, and it wins -
   "Modifier la publication" where the breadcrumb says "Modifier".

   **The breadcrumb keeps all its levels, including the current page.** The
   first version removed that last level, on the grounds that the strip above
   already says it. It was wrong in a way only the rendered page shows:
   fourteen pages out of nineteen have exactly two levels, so the breadcrumb
   became a single word - the section's name, which the side menu already
   shows. A breadcrumb with a single item is not a breadcrumb, it is a label.

   The last level is marked as current and written in the body colour, the
   levels above it in grey: the path reads as "where I come from, where I am"
   without leaning on the strip above. Weight stays at medium - emphasis is
   still the title's job.

   The edges of both strips read `--aurora-page-margin`, like `<main>`. On a
   phone, where only the breadcrumb is left, it therefore lines up exactly
   with the first card - this is the offset the two files each kept on their
   own side, sixteen pixels here against eight below.

   `backLink`: the page draws its own way back (AppPageBar). Below `lg`, the
   parent level then reads as plain text rather than a second link to the
   same place.

   `strict_variables` is on, so the guard below matters: asking an empty list
   for `.label` raises an error instead of rendering nothing.
#}
{% set trail = crumbs|default([]) %}
{% set pageTitle = title|default(trail ? (trail|last).label : '') %}

<header class="lg:sticky lg:top-0 lg:z-20 shrink-0">
    {# Reserved for large screens, all of it: below that, the menu is a drawer
       that carries its own bar with the logo, the search and the bell, and
       this strip would only hold a title between two hidden groups there -
       forty-eight pixels of border. #}
    <div class="aurora-page-x hidden h-12 items-center gap-3 border-b border-line bg-surface lg:flex">
        {# The only control that collapses the menu, at the top left of the
           page - not in the menu it hides. A button that lives in what it hides
           needs a second copy elsewhere to bring it back, which Aurora had.
           Then come the browser's arrows, brought into the page - only when
           the suite runs as an installed application, which has no toolbar of
           its own. In a browser tab they doubled the ones right above them
           (UI audit of 07/10/2026). #}
        <div class="flex shrink-0 items-center gap-1">
            <div {{ vue_component('core/suite/sidemenu/AppSidemenuToggle', {
                collapsedPath: path('suite_general_profile_sidemenu_collapsed'),
            }) }}></div>
            <div class="hidden border-l border-line pl-1 [@media(display-mode:standalone)]:block">
                <div {{ vue_component('core/suite/sidemenu/AppTopbarNav') }}></div>
            </div>
        </div>

        {# `h2` and not `h1`: two pages already give their `h1` to what they are
           about - a document's title, a section heading next to its Nouveau
           button - and a second `h1` here would leave the document outline
           with two tops. #}
        <h2 class="min-w-0 flex-1 truncate text-base font-semibold leading-tight text-primary">{{ pageTitle }}</h2>

        {# Search, notifications and account, far right. They used to live in
           the navigation menu, above the filter, which put controls unrelated
           to navigation into what navigates. #}
        <div class="flex shrink-0 items-center gap-1">
            <div {{ vue_component('core/suite/sidemenu/AppTopbarActions', {
                notificationsListPath: path('suite_notifications_list'),
                notificationsMarkReadPath: path_template('suite_notifications_mark_read', {id: '__id__'}),
                notificationsMarkAllReadPath: path('suite_notifications_mark_all_read'),
                notificationsDeletePath: path_template('suite_notifications_delete', {id: '__id__'}),
                notificationsDeleteAllPath: path('suite_notifications_delete_all'),
                userName: app.user.name,
                userEmail: app.user.email,
                userPhotoUrl: aurora_profile_photo_url(app.user) ?? '',
                mailpitUrl: aurora_is_dev() ? 'http://127.0.0.1:8025' : '',
                profilePath: path('suite_general_profile'),
                preferencesPath: path('suite_general_profile_sidemenu'),
                logoutPath: path('suite_platform_logout'),
                logoutCsrf: csrf_token('logout'),
            }) }}></div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# The breadcrumb, at every size. On a phone it is the whole header, and the
       levels above the last two fade out: there is no second strip to fall
       back to, and a deep path would push the page it names off the screen. #}
    {% if trail %}
        <nav class="aurora-page-x flex h-8 items-center gap-1.5 border-b border-line bg-surface text-xs" aria-label="{{ 'suite.nav.breadcrumb'|trans }}">
            {% for crumb in trail %}
                {% set isNarrowVisible = loop.index >= (trail|length - 1) %}
                {# A separator goes with the level before it: on a phone, where
                   that level is hidden, a visible one opened the path with a
                   stray « / Clients ». #}
                {% set isSeparatorNarrowVisible = (loop.index - 1) >= (trail|length - 1) %}
                {% if not loop.first %}
                    <span class="select-none shrink-0 text-muted {% if not isSeparatorNarrowVisible %}hidden sm:inline{% endif %}">/</span>
                {% endif %}
                <span class="min-w-0 {% if not isNarrowVisible %}hidden sm:inline{% endif %}">
                    {% if backLink|default(false) and loop.index == trail|length - 1 and crumb.href is defined and crumb.href %}
                        {# The page has its own way back to this level, a few
                           pixels below: on a phone and a tablet the two were
                           the same link twice. The name stays, as context. #}
                        <a href="{{ crumb.href }}" class="hidden truncate text-secondary transition-colors hover:text-primary lg:block">{{ crumb.label }}</a>
                        <span class="block truncate text-secondary lg:hidden">{{ crumb.label }}</span>
                    {% elseif crumb.href is defined and crumb.href %}
                        <a href="{{ crumb.href }}" class="block truncate text-secondary transition-colors hover:text-primary">{{ crumb.label }}</a>
                    {% elseif loop.last %}
                        <span class="block truncate font-medium text-primary" aria-current="page">{{ crumb.label }}</span>
                    {% else %}
                        <span class="block truncate text-secondary">{{ crumb.label }}</span>
                    {% endif %}
                </span>
            {% endfor %}
        </nav>
    {% endif %}
</header>
